  "name": "AMINO ACID QUANTITATIVE PLASMA",
  "code": "1028",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"220\" data-end=\"357\">The <strong data-start=\"224\" data-end=\"263\">Amino Acid Quantitative Plasma Test</strong> measures the levels of various <strong data-start=\"295\" data-end=\"310\">amino acids</strong> in the bloodstream. It is primarily used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"358\" data-end=\"695\">\n<li data-start=\"358\" data-end=\"526\">\n<p data-start=\"360\" data-end=\"410\">Diagnose <strong data-start=\"369\" data-end=\"400\">inborn errors of metabolism</strong>, such as:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"413\" data-end=\"526\">\n<li data-start=\"413\" data-end=\"440\">\n<p data-start=\"415\" data-end=\"440\"><strong data-start=\"415\" data-end=\"440\">Phenylketonuria (PKU)</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"443\" data-end=\"481\">\n<p data-start=\"445\" data-end=\"481\"><strong data-start=\"445\" data-end=\"481\">Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD)</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"484\" data-end=\"504\">\n<p data-start=\"486\" data-end=\"504\"><strong data-start=\"486\" data-end=\"504\">Homocystinuria</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"507\" data-end=\"526\">\n<p data-start=\"509\" data-end=\"526\"><strong data-start=\"509\" data-end=\"526\">Citrullinemia</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"527\" data-end=\"582\">\n<p data-start=\"529\" data-end=\"582\">Monitor patients with <strong data-start=\"551\" data-end=\"580\">known metabolic disorders</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"583\" data-end=\"695\">\n<p data-start=\"585\" data-end=\"695\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"594\" data-end=\"622\">nutritional deficiencies</strong>, malabsorption, or liver/kidney dysfunction in certain clinical settings</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"697\" data-end=\"864\">Each amino acid has a distinct clinical implication when elevated, and patterns of abnormalities often point to specific <strong data-start=\"818\" data-end=\"863\">metabolic pathways or enzyme deficiencies</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Amino Acid Elevated</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Associated Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance / Notes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phenylalanine</strong></td>\n<td>Phenylketonuria (PKU)</td>\n<td>Intellectual disability if untreated; enzyme: phenylalanine hydroxylase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Leucine, Isoleucine, Valine</strong></td>\n<td>Maple Syrup Urine Disease (MSUD)</td>\n<td>Newborn crisis; sweet-smelling urine; neurotoxicity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Homocysteine</strong></td>\n<td>Homocystinuria</td>\n<td>Vascular disease risk, lens dislocation, developmental delay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Citrulline</strong></td>\n<td>Citrullinemia</td>\n<td>Urea cycle disorder; risk of hyperammonemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Argininosuccinic acid</strong></td>\n<td>Argininosuccinic aciduria</td>\n<td>Urea cycle disorder; leads to lethargy, vomiting, coma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tyrosine</strong></td>\n<td>Tyrosinemia (Type I, II)</td>\n<td>Liver/kidney dysfunction, neurologic complications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Methionine</strong></td>\n<td>Homocystinuria or Liver Dysfunction</td>\n<td>Marker for disturbed methylation/metabolism pathways</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ornithine</strong></td>\n<td>HHH Syndrome (Hyperornithinemia-Hyperammonemia-Homocitrullinuria)</td>\n<td>Rare urea cycle disorder causing ammonia buildup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Glycine</strong></td>\n<td>Nonketotic Hyperglycinemia</td>\n<td>Severe neonatal encephalopathy; glycine cleavage defect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Histidine</strong></td>\n<td>Histidinemia</td>\n<td>Usually benign; rare metabolic disorder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alanine</strong></td>\n<td>May increase in liver disease or pyruvate metabolism errors</td>\n<td>Non-specific; can be secondary to metabolic or hepatic stress</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"697\" data-end=\"864\">&nbsp;</p>",
